=begin
      author: Fizzlesticks
        name: playsongs
        game: gemstone
        tags: bard, tambourine, performance

  Background script that keeps Fizzlesticks playing an instrument.

  Gets the instrument from the backpack, plays it, then watches for the
  game's "performance ended" messages. When one shows up, it pauses briefly
  and plays again.

  Each song cycle (from "you lift your instrument and begin..." to one of
  the "song ended" messages) is timed, and any PLAY skill rank-up messages
  noticed along the way are counted. Per gswiki.play.net/Instrument_learning
  that message fires once per rank out of 20 total, each taking exponentially
  longer than the last (rank 5 ~3h48m, rank 10 ~18h12m, rank 15 ~50h, rank
  20/Adept ~115h18m) -- so a low count over a few hours is expected, not a
  sign of missed messages. Every 5 completed songs it also runs PLAY SKILL
  directly and saves the actual "Your current skill level in X instruments
  is Y" results as a second confirmation, shown in playsongs stats. Stats
  are tracked per instrument in a small YAML file at
  data/<game>/<character>/playsongs_stats.yaml.

  A song that ends in under 285 seconds (MIN_SONG_SECONDS minus a
  SONG_LENGTH_TOLERANCE grace period) is treated as aborted -- e.g.
  interrupted before it could finish naturally -- and isn't counted toward
  songs_played/total_seconds, though any rank-ups noticed during it still
  are. The grace period exists because a genuine completion can land a
  few seconds under the ~300s target.

  Auto-pauses (no command needed) for as long as ;eloot sell is running
  (not eloot alone), and resumes once it finishes -- see EXCLUSIVE_SCRIPTS
  to add others. Any pause -- that auto-pause or a manual "playsongs
  pause" -- stows the instrument, and resuming gets it back out.

  Bigshot auto-pause is opt-in per character, since it only matters if the
  instrument occupies a hand you need free while hunting (spellcasting
  classes) -- a one-handed instrument doesn't bother a bard. Turn it on
  with:
          ;vars set playsongs_pause_for_bigshot=true
  When on, this only pauses while Bigshot is actually in a hunting-like
  state (per its own $bigshot_status) -- not the whole time Bigshot
  happens to be running, so it stays playing while Bigshot is just resting
  in town between hunts.

  Usage:  ;playsongs                (tambourine, cycling through the styles)
          ;playsongs lute           (play a different instrument instead)
          ;playsongs tambourine fast   (pin one style: soft, fast or slow; no cycling)

  Styles: unless one is pinned on the command line, each completed song moves
  on to the next style (soft, fast, slow, soft, ...), the way the wiki's author
  "cycled through the different styles so that I ultimately played each one
  about the same amount of time" (https://gswiki.play.net/Instrument_learning).
  The position is saved per instrument, so it carries across restarts. Type
  PLAY on its own in the game to see what an instrument offers.

  Tuning: an out-of-tune instrument plays a random message instead of the one
  asked for, and may slow learning (same wiki page), and it can't tune itself.
  So before the first song of each day (TUNE_INTERVAL_SECONDS) it sends
  TUNE <instrument> and prints whatever the game answers. "playsongs tune"
  forces one before the next song. Needs 4 ranks in the instrument's class.

  Set a persistent default instrument (used whenever no instrument is
  given on the command line) with:
          ;vars set instrument=lute
  A command-line instrument always overrides that setting -- ";playsongs
  tambourine" plays tambourine for that run even if instrument is set to
  lute in ;vars.

  Commands -- "stats" and "help" work either way (;playsongs stats or just
  typing playsongs stats), including before the script has ever been
  started. "pause"/"resume" need it already running, so use the no-
  semicolon form for those while it's going in the background:
          playsongs stats           - print stats for every instrument
                                       tracked so far
          playsongs pause           - stop starting new songs after the
                                       current one finishes
          playsongs resume          - resume after a pause
          playsongs tune            - tune the instrument before the next song
          playsongs help            - show this help

  Stop:   ;kill playsongs
=end

require 'yaml'
require 'fileutils'
require 'time'

module Playsongs

  COMMANDS = %w[stats help pause resume tune].freeze

  # @param word [String, nil]
  # @return [String, nil] the COMMANDS entry word is an unambiguous
  #   (3+ char) prefix of, e.g. "res"/"resum" -> "resume", or nil
  def self.match_command(word)
    return nil unless word

    w = word.downcase
    COMMANDS.find { |c| w.length >= 3 && c.start_with?(w) }
  end

  # vars[1] doubles as both the instrument name (";playsongs lute") and a
  # command (";playsongs help") -- resolve which one it is before deciding
  # INSTRUMENT, so "help"/"stats"/etc. never end up as a literal instrument
  # name (e.g. when the script isn't running yet, so the UpstreamHook
  # trigger below -- which only fires for the no-semicolon typed form --
  # never gets a chance to handle it).
  RAW_FIRST_ARG = Script.current.vars[1]
  EARLY_COMMAND = match_command(RAW_FIRST_ARG)

  # Persistent default instrument, set with: ;vars set instrument=lute
  # A command-line arg (";playsongs lute") always wins over this when given;
  # this is only the fallback used when none is passed. Hardcoded
  # "tambourine" is the last resort if neither is set.
  DEFAULT_INSTRUMENT = (UserVars.instrument || "tambourine").to_s.downcase

  INSTRUMENT = (EARLY_COMMAND ? DEFAULT_INSTRUMENT : (RAW_FIRST_ARG || DEFAULT_INSTRUMENT)).downcase

  # The styles to cycle through, in order (see the header).
  STYLES = %w[soft fast slow].freeze

  # A style given on the command line pins it for the whole run (no cycling);
  # nothing, "cycle" or "all" cycles through STYLES.
  requested_style = Script.current.vars[2].to_s.downcase
  PINNED_STYLE =
    if requested_style.empty? || %w[cycle all].include?(requested_style)
      nil
    elsif STYLES.include?(requested_style)
      requested_style
    else
      echo "Unknown style '#{requested_style}', cycling instead. Valid styles: #{STYLES.join(', ')}, or cycle."
      nil
    end

  # Song-start lines. The first is the one seen live for the tambourine; the next
  # three are the soft / fast / slow starts from
  # https://gswiki.play.net/Instrument_messaging_percussion; the last two are the
  # drum's (soft, then fast and slow, seen live 11/11).
  # Only used to time the song from when it really began.
  START_REGEX = Regexp.union(
    /You lift your .+? and begin to tap out a beat\./,
    /You softly shake your .+?, starting a gentle and lulling rhythm\./,
    /You test your .+? with a gentle thump, then raise your head with a smile and begin a rapid beat\./,
    /You slowly raise your .+? and begin to tap out a slow, soothing beat\./,
    /You begin to \w+ tap on your .+?\./,
    /You begin to beat a .+? rhythm on your .+?\./
  )

  # The game's answer to a style an instrument doesn't have.
  INVALID_STYLE_REGEX = /That isn't a valid style for a/

  # What TUNE answers when it worked, both seen live on Fizzlestiks 11/11:
  #   tambourine: "You carefully check your tambourine, making sure the tiny
  #                cymbals are firmly in place and in good condition."
  #               (also the percussion line on the wiki's messaging page)
  #   drum:       "You carefully tune your drum, tapping the head to make sure it
  #                has the right tension."
  # The last alternative is a loose catch for other instruments' wording ("...is
  # now in tune"), worded so a failure like "Tune what?" doesn't count.
  TUNED_REGEX = Regexp.union(
    /You carefully check your .+?, making sure/,
    /You carefully tune your .+?, tapping/,
    /\byou (?:carefully )?tune\b|\btuned\b|\bin tune\b/i
  )

  # Seen live 11/11 for a flute with too few ranks: "You are not skilled enough
  # with that instrument to tune it." Retrying every hour would be pointless, so
  # this backs off to the normal daily interval.
  TUNE_UNSKILLED_REGEX = /not skilled enough with that instrument to tune it/

  # Per https://gswiki.play.net/Instrument_learning, this message fires once
  # per rank (not per skill tick), and there are only 20 ranks total, with
  # each one taking exponentially longer than the last (rank 5 ~3h48m,
  # rank 10 ~18h12m, rank 15 ~50h, rank 20/Adept ~115h18m). So a low count
  # over a few hours is expected, not a sign this is missing messages --
  # it just won't fire often. Kept in sync with the periodic PLAY SKILL
  # check (SKILL_LEVEL_REGEX/maybe_check_skill) as a second confirmation of
  # the same authoritative tier, shown in playsongs stats.
  RANKUP_REGEX = /You feel a sense of accomplishment as you continue practicing your .+?!/

  SKILL_LEVEL_REGEX = /Your current skill level in (.+?) instruments is (\w+)\.?/i

  # Some instruments (e.g. piccolo) need both hands, so having the right
  # hand free for a weapon per ensure_in_left_hand isn't always possible --
  # this catches the game telling us so and reacts by stowing the right
  # hand and retrying, rather than needing a hardcoded list of which
  # instruments are two-handed.
  TWO_HANDED_REGEX = /requires two hands to play, try emptying your right hand\./

  # "your .+?" instead of the instrument name so this still matches regardless
  # of what is being played -- including multi-word names ("rosewood drum", "modwir
  # piccolo"), which a single-word \S+ silently failed to match. "you"/"You"
  # scopes it to your own performance so it won't trigger off other bards
  # finishing theirs. Each new instrument can bring its own ending line.
  STOP_REGEX = Regexp.union(
    /As the last whispered note fades from the stilled air, you slowly smile and put your hand over your .+?, bowing with a flourish\./,
    # Slow. The game prints this as two sentences with TWO spaces between them
    # ("...fades away.  You look up from your tambourine and smile."), so the old
    # pattern, which had one space, never matched -- slow songs always timed out.
    # Ending at the first sentence avoids depending on the spacing at all.
    /The music moves slower and slower until finally, with a last hovering beat, it fades away\./,
    /The air suddenly stills as you halt your whirling rhythm as suddenly as you began, silencing your .+?\./,
    /You complete the last beat with a flourish, lowering your .+?\./,
    /With quiet grace, you allow your .+?'s melody to drop off, ever softer, until there is nothing but the intense silence left in its wake\./,
    # Confirmed live: without this, play_and_track's read loop never sees a
    # STOP_REGEX match and just hangs forever after a song actually ended --
    # playsongs looked "running" but never played again.
    /You bring your quiet performance to a close with three descending harmonics; these last notes ring on, barely heard, as you let your arms fall to your sides\./,
    # Drum, seen live 11/11 (soft / slow / fast):
    #   "You let the soft beats on your rosewood drum trail away into silence."
    #   "You let the slow beats on your rosewood drum gradually come to rest."
    #   "You bring your rapid drumming to an abrupt halt on your rosewood drum."
    /You let the .+? beats on your .+? (?:trail away into silence|gradually come to rest)\./,
    /You bring your rapid \w+ to an abrupt halt on your .+?\./
  )

  # Backstop for the next ending message nobody has seen yet: a song is 5-5.5
  # minutes, so if neither a known ending nor a new START line shows up for this
  # long, stop waiting instead of sitting in hand doing nothing until something
  # else kills the script (that is how the drum stalled for 11 minutes).
  SONG_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 420

  # How long a tuning is trusted before the next one. The wiki only says "from time
  # to time"; a day is the schedule asked for.
  TUNE_INTERVAL_SECONDS = 86_400

  # A TUNE whose answer wasn't recognised is retried after this, not every song.
  TUNE_RETRY_SECONDS = 3600

  RESTART_PAUSE_SECONDS = 3
  PAUSE_POLL_SECONDS = 1
  SKILL_CHECK_INTERVAL = 5   # run PLAY SKILL every this many completed songs
  MIN_SONG_SECONDS = 300     # ~5 min per gswiki.play.net/Instrument_learning -- a real song's target length
  SONG_LENGTH_TOLERANCE = 15 # grace period below MIN_SONG_SECONDS -- timing/detection can land a few
                              # seconds early on a genuine completion (e.g. 299s), which shouldn't get
                              # treated the same as an actually-interrupted song

  # [rank, cumulative seconds] checkpoints from
  # https://gswiki.play.net/Instrument_learning -- (0, 0) added as a
  # starting point so there's a full curve to interpolate/extrapolate
  # against, not just the four wiki data points.
  MILESTONES = [[0, 0], [5, 13_680], [10, 65_520], [15, 180_000], [20, 415_080]].freeze

  TIER_RANGES = { 'novice' => 1..5, 'apprentice' => 6..13, 'journeyman' => 14..19, 'adept' => 20..20 }.freeze

  # Very rough instrument -> PLAY SKILL category guesses, just enough to
  # cross-reference the estimate below against the real tier from
  # maybe_check_skill. Add more as needed.
  CATEGORY_HINTS = {
    'percussion' => %w[tambourine drum bongo cymbal],
    'woodwind' => %w[flute whistle fife piccolo],
    'stringed' => %w[lute harp mandolin fiddle guitar lyre],
    'brass' => %w[horn trumpet trombone]
  }.freeze

  # @return [Float] estimated rank (0-20) for total seconds played, via
  #   linear interpolation between MILESTONES, clamped at 20
  def self.estimated_rank(seconds)
    return 20.0 if seconds >= MILESTONES.last[1]

    MILESTONES.each_cons(2) do |(r1, s1), (r2, s2)|
      next unless seconds.between?(s1, s2)

      return r1 + (seconds - s1).to_f / (s2 - s1) * (r2 - r1)
    end
    0.0
  end

  # @return [Integer] estimated cumulative seconds needed to reach a rank
  def self.time_for_rank(rank)
    return MILESTONES.last[1] if rank >= MILESTONES.last[0]

    MILESTONES.each_cons(2) do |(r1, s1), (r2, s2)|
      next unless rank.between?(r1, r2)

      return (s1 + (rank - r1).to_f / (r2 - r1) * (s2 - s1)).round
    end
    0
  end

  # @return [String, nil] tier name ("novice", "apprentice", ...) for an
  #   estimated (possibly fractional) rank
  def self.tier_for_rank(rank)
    TIER_RANGES.find { |_tier, range| range.cover?(rank.round) }&.first
  end

  # @param instrument_name [String]
  # @return [String, nil] best-guess PLAY SKILL category name for it
  def self.category_for(instrument_name)
    CATEGORY_HINTS.find { |_category, names| names.any? { |n| instrument_name.include?(n) } }&.first
  end

  # Whether Bigshot hunting needs a free hand this instrument would
  # otherwise occupy is character-dependent -- a one-handed instrument (e.g.
  # tambourine) doesn't get in a bard's way, but a sorcerer/wizard needs a
  # hand open for spellcasting. Per-character opt-in, same mechanism as the
  # default instrument: ;vars set playsongs_pause_for_bigshot=true
  PAUSE_FOR_BIGSHOT = UserVars.playsongs_pause_for_bigshot.to_s =~ /^(true|yes|1)$/i ? true : false

  # Bigshot sets the global $bigshot_status as it goes -- :ready, :resting,
  # and :rest_stop mean it isn't actively hunting/casting right now (e.g.
  # resting in town square), so there's no need to keep a hand free for
  # those. Anything else (:hunting, :boundary_building, ...) is treated as
  # needing the hand, same as an unrecognized future status would be --
  # safer to pause and miss a beat than to sit there uncastable mid-fight.
  BIGSHOT_IDLE_STATUSES = %i[ready resting rest_stop].freeze

  # Scripts that need your hands/attention -- playsongs auto-pauses (same as
  # a manual "playsongs pause") for as long as a running script matches one
  # of these, and auto-resumes once it finishes, no command needed either
  # way. arg_pattern nil means any invocation of that script name matches;
  # status_check, if given, is an extra proc that must return true for the
  # match to actually count as blocking (checked only once a name/arg match
  # is already found).
  EXCLUSIVE_SCRIPTS = [
    { name: 'eloot', arg_pattern: /sell/i } # only "eloot sell", not eloot alone
  ] + (PAUSE_FOR_BIGSHOT ? [{
    name: 'bigshot',
    arg_pattern: nil,
    status_check: -> { !$bigshot_status.nil? && !BIGSHOT_IDLE_STATUSES.include?($bigshot_status) }
  }] : [])

  STATS_FILE = File.join(DATA_DIR, XMLData.game, Char.name, 'playsongs_stats.yaml')

  @paused = false
  @pause_mutex = Mutex.new
  @song_count = 0
  @blocking_script = nil
  @was_paused = false
  @tune_requested = false

  def self.pause!
    @pause_mutex.synchronize { @paused = true }
  end

  def self.resume!
    @pause_mutex.synchronize { @paused = false }
  end

  def self.paused?
    @pause_mutex.synchronize { @paused }
  end

  # Set from the UpstreamHook thread ("playsongs tune"); the main loop does the
  # actual TUNE, since fput needs the script's own thread.
  def self.request_tune!
    @pause_mutex.synchronize { @tune_requested = true }
  end

  def self.tune_requested?
    @pause_mutex.synchronize { @tune_requested }
  end

  # @return [String, nil] "name arg1 arg2..." for the first running script
  #   matching an EXCLUSIVE_SCRIPTS entry, or nil if none match
  def self.blocking_script
    running = Script.list
    EXCLUSIVE_SCRIPTS.each do |spec|
      match = running.find do |s|
        s.name == spec[:name] &&
          (spec[:arg_pattern].nil? || s.vars.any? { |v| v.to_s =~ spec[:arg_pattern] })
      end
      next unless match
      next if spec[:status_check] && !spec[:status_check].call

      # vars[0] is the whole args string joined back together (e.g. "sell"),
      # and vars[1..] are the individual tokens -- using the full vars array
      # here double-counts a single-word arg ("eloot sell sell").
      return [match.name, *match.vars[1..]].join(' ')
    end
    nil
  end

  def self.load_stats
    return {} unless File.exist?(STATS_FILE)

    YAML.load_file(STATS_FILE) || {}
  end

  def self.save_stats(stats)
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.dirname(STATS_FILE))
    File.write(STATS_FILE, stats.to_yaml)
  end

  def self.record_song(duration_seconds, rank_ups, style = nil)
    stats = load_stats
    entry = stats[INSTRUMENT] ||= { 'songs_played' => 0, 'rank_ups' => 0, 'total_seconds' => 0 }
    entry['songs_played'] += 1
    entry['rank_ups'] += rank_ups
    entry['total_seconds'] += duration_seconds.round
    if style
      by_style = (entry['style_seconds'] ||= {})
      by_style[style] = by_style[style].to_i + duration_seconds.round
    end
    # next song uses the next style in the cycle (a pinned style never moves on)
    entry['style_index'] = entry['style_index'].to_i + 1 unless PINNED_STYLE
    entry['last_played'] = Time.now.to_s
    save_stats(stats)
    entry
  end

  # Rank-ups are a real skill event even if the song itself gets aborted
  # early, so they're still worth keeping -- just without touching
  # songs_played/total_seconds for a song that didn't really happen.
  # advance_style: also move the cycle on a style. For a song that didn't count
  # but shouldn't be repeated (a timeout, or a style the instrument rejects) --
  # otherwise the cycle would sit on that style forever. An aborted song leaves it
  # alone, so the same style is tried again.
  def self.record_rank_ups_only(rank_ups, advance_style: false)
    advance = advance_style && !PINNED_STYLE
    return if rank_ups.zero? && !advance

    stats = load_stats
    entry = stats[INSTRUMENT] ||= { 'songs_played' => 0, 'rank_ups' => 0, 'total_seconds' => 0 }
    entry['rank_ups'] += rank_ups
    entry['style_index'] = entry['style_index'].to_i + 1 if advance
    save_stats(stats)
    entry
  end

  # The style for the next song: the pinned one, or the next in the cycle (the
  # position is saved per instrument, so it carries across restarts).
  def self.pick_style
    return PINNED_STYLE if PINNED_STYLE

    STYLES[load_stats.dig(INSTRUMENT, 'style_index').to_i % STYLES.length]
  end

  def self.parse_time(value)
    value && Time.parse(value.to_s)
  rescue ArgumentError
    nil
  end

  # Due when asked for, or when it has never been tuned / was last tuned more than
  # TUNE_INTERVAL_SECONDS ago -- but not again within TUNE_RETRY_SECONDS of an
  # attempt whose answer wasn't recognised, or within a day of "not skilled enough".
  def self.tune_due?
    return true if tune_requested?

    entry = load_stats[INSTRUMENT] || {}
    attempt = parse_time(entry['last_tune_attempt'])
    wait = entry['tune_unskilled'] ? TUNE_INTERVAL_SECONDS : TUNE_RETRY_SECONDS
    return false if attempt && Time.now - attempt < wait

    tuned = parse_time(entry['last_tuned'])
    tuned.nil? || Time.now - tuned >= TUNE_INTERVAL_SECONDS
  end

  # Sends TUNE and returns the game's answer, minus blank and XML-tag lines.
  # The capture has to START on one of the known replies: starting on the first
  # line of anything (/\S/) grabbed whatever unrelated line arrived first (room
  # exits, "You feel more courageous.", song-manager spam), and the real reply
  # fell outside the capture -- so a successful tune was reported as
  # unrecognised and retried hourly (11/11 log). No reply within the timeout
  # comes back as an empty list.
  TUNE_REPLY_REGEX = Regexp.union(TUNED_REGEX, TUNE_UNSKILLED_REGEX, TWO_HANDED_REGEX)

  def self.send_tune
    waitrt?
    lines = Array(Lich::Util.issue_command("tune my #{INSTRUMENT}", TUNE_REPLY_REGEX, /<prompt/, include_end: false, timeout: 8))
    lines.map { |l| l.to_s.strip }.reject { |l| l.empty? || l.start_with?('<') }
  end

  def self.tune_instrument
    lines = send_tune
    if lines.any? { |l| l =~ TWO_HANDED_REGEX || l =~ /two hands/i }
      fput "store right"
      fput "stow right" if checkright
      lines = send_tune
    end

    tuned = lines.any? { |l| l =~ TUNED_REGEX }
    stats = load_stats
    entry = stats[INSTRUMENT] ||= { 'songs_played' => 0, 'rank_ups' => 0, 'total_seconds' => 0 }
    unskilled = lines.any? { |l| l =~ TUNE_UNSKILLED_REGEX }
    entry['last_tune_attempt'] = Time.now.to_s
    entry['last_tuned'] = Time.now.to_s if tuned
    entry['tune_unskilled'] = unskilled
    save_stats(stats)
    @pause_mutex.synchronize { @tune_requested = false }

    shown = lines.first(4).join(' | ')
    if tuned
      Lich::Messaging.msg("bold", "[Playsongs] Tuned #{INSTRUMENT}. Game said: #{shown}")
    elsif unskilled
      Lich::Messaging.msg("bold", "[Playsongs] Can't tune #{INSTRUMENT} yet -- not enough ranks in its class. Playing it untuned (the game may pick a random message); will try again tomorrow. Game said: #{shown}")
    else
      Lich::Messaging.msg("bold", "[Playsongs] Sent TUNE for #{INSTRUMENT}, but the answer isn't one I recognise, so I'll try again in #{TUNE_RETRY_SECONDS / 60} min. " \
                                  "Game said: #{shown.empty? ? '(nothing)' : shown}")
    end
    waitrt?
  end

  def self.maybe_tune
    tune_instrument if tune_due?
  end

  # @param total_seconds [Integer]
  # @return [String] "1h 23m 45s" (omitting leading zero units, but always
  #   showing seconds)
  def self.format_duration(total_seconds)
    hours, remainder = total_seconds.divmod(3600)
    minutes, seconds = remainder.divmod(60)

    parts = []
    parts << "#{hours}h" if hours > 0
    parts << "#{minutes}m" if hours > 0 || minutes > 0
    parts << "#{seconds}s"
    parts.join(' ')
  end

  # Prints every instrument's tracked stats to the local window.
  def self.show_stats
    stats = load_stats

    respond ''
    respond '--- Playsongs Stats ---'
    if stats.empty?
      respond 'No stats recorded yet.'
    else
      stats.each do |instrument, entry|
        songs = entry['songs_played'].to_i
        seconds = entry['total_seconds'].to_i
        avg = songs > 0 ? (seconds / songs.to_f).round(1) : 0
        respond "#{instrument}: #{songs} songs, #{entry['rank_ups'].to_i} rank-ups, #{format_duration(seconds)} played (avg #{avg}s/song), last played #{entry['last_played'] || 'never'}"

        by_style = entry['style_seconds']
        if by_style.is_a?(Hash) && !by_style.empty?
          respond "  By style (since style cycling began): " + STYLES.map { |st| "#{st} #{format_duration(by_style[st].to_i)}" }.join(', ')
        end
        respond "  Last tuned: #{entry['last_tuned']}" if entry['last_tuned']

        levels = entry['skill_levels']
        if levels && !levels.empty?
          levels_str = levels.map { |category, level| "#{category}: #{level}" }.join(', ')
          respond "  PLAY SKILL (as of #{entry['skill_checked_at']}): #{levels_str}"
        end

        # Just for fun -- https://gswiki.play.net/Instrument_learning-based
        # estimate of rank from tracked hours alone, cross-checked against
        # the real tier from the PLAY SKILL check above. seconds here only
        # covers time this script tracked, so it'll almost always look like
        # you're "ahead of schedule" -- that gap is prior manual practice
        # and/or aborted songs this script never counted, not extra-fast
        # learning.
        est_rank = estimated_rank(seconds)
        respond "  Estimate from tracked time alone: rank ~#{est_rank.round(1)} (#{tier_for_rank(est_rank)})"

        category = category_for(instrument)
        actual_tier = levels && category ? levels[category] : nil
        if actual_tier && TIER_RANGES[actual_tier.downcase]
          expected_seconds = time_for_rank(TIER_RANGES[actual_tier.downcase].first)
          diff = expected_seconds - seconds
          if diff > 0
            respond "  That's #{format_duration(diff)} less than the estimate for reaching #{actual_tier} (~#{format_duration(expected_seconds)}) -- there was likely some practice time and aborted songs not tracked here."
          else
            respond "  Right on pace (or ahead) of the estimate for reaching #{actual_tier}."
          end
        end
      end
    end
    respond ''
  end

  # Prints available commands to the local window.
  def self.show_help
    respond ''
    respond '--- Playsongs Help ---'
    respond "Keeps playing an instrument (#{PINNED_STYLE ? "#{PINNED_STYLE} style, pinned" : 'cycling soft / fast / slow'}) in a loop, timing each song and counting rank-ups."
    respond "Also runs PLAY SKILL every #{SKILL_CHECK_INTERVAL} completed songs and saves the real skill level as a second confirmation of the rank-up count."
    respond "Auto-pauses while eloot sell is running (not eloot alone), no command needed."
    respond "Bigshot auto-pause: #{PAUSE_FOR_BIGSHOT ? 'ON' : 'off'} (toggle with ;vars set playsongs_pause_for_bigshot=true/false) -- when on, only pauses while Bigshot is actually hunting, not while it's resting in town."
    respond "Any pause stows the instrument; resuming gets it back out."
    respond ''
    respond 'Commands (typed directly, no leading semicolon):'
    respond '  playsongs stats    - print stats for every instrument tracked so far'
    respond '  playsongs pause    - stop starting new songs after the current one finishes'
    respond '  playsongs resume   - resume after a pause'
    respond '  playsongs tune     - tune the instrument before the next song (it also tunes by itself once a day)'
    respond '  playsongs help     - show this help'
    respond ''
    respond "Default instrument: set with ;vars set instrument=<name> (currently: #{DEFAULT_INSTRUMENT}). A command-line instrument always overrides it."
    respond ''
    respond 'Stop the script entirely with: ;kill playsongs'
    respond ''
  end

  def self.save_skill_levels(levels)
    return if levels.empty?

    stats = load_stats
    entry = stats[INSTRUMENT] ||= { 'songs_played' => 0, 'rank_ups' => 0, 'total_seconds' => 0 }
    entry['skill_levels'] = levels
    entry['skill_checked_at'] = Time.now.to_s
    save_stats(stats)
  end

  # Every SKILL_CHECK_INTERVAL completed songs, runs PLAY SKILL and captures
  # its response (e.g. "Your current skill level in percussion instruments
  # is Apprentice.") -- this is the game's own authoritative skill report,
  # unlike the rough RANKUP_REGEX counter, and gets saved for playsongs
  # stats to display.
  def self.maybe_check_skill(song_count)
    return unless (song_count % SKILL_CHECK_INTERVAL).zero?

    echo "Periodic PLAY SKILL check (every #{SKILL_CHECK_INTERVAL} songs) --"
    lines = Lich::Util.issue_command("play skill", SKILL_LEVEL_REGEX, /<prompt/, include_end: false, silent: true)

    levels = {}
    Array(lines).each { |line| levels[$1.downcase] = $2 if line =~ SKILL_LEVEL_REGEX }
    save_skill_levels(levels)
  end

  # No "from my backpack" -- the game sometimes auto-stows the instrument
  # into whatever container has room (e.g. when both hands are needed for
  # something else), not necessarily the backpack, so this needs to find it
  # wherever it landed.
  def self.get_instrument
    fput "get my #{INSTRUMENT}"
  end

  def self.stow_instrument
    fput "stow my #{INSTRUMENT}" if checkleft(INSTRUMENT) || checkright(INSTRUMENT)
  end

  # INSTRUMENT belongs in the left hand so the right hand stays free for a
  # weapon. Fixes wherever it currently is: GETs it if it's not in hand at
  # all, or swaps hands if it landed in the right hand (other scripts love
  # to put things away, not always into the same hand).
  # @return [Boolean] true if it was already correctly in the left hand
  def self.ensure_in_left_hand
    fput "glance"

    if checkleft(INSTRUMENT)
      true
    elsif checkright(INSTRUMENT)
      echo "#{INSTRUMENT} is in my right hand -- swapping so my right hand is free for a weapon."
      fput "swap"
      false
    else
      # Neither hand holds it. GET fails silently if both hands are already
      # full of something else (e.g. a wand left over from Bigshot's wave
      # routine, alongside a weapon) -- happened after resting picked back
      # up with hands never cleared since the last combat cycle. Only the
      # left hand needs to be free for this; leave the right hand (and
      # whatever it's holding) alone.
      if checkleft
        echo "Putting #{checkleft} away to make room for #{INSTRUMENT}."
        # STORE, not STOW -- so a readied weapon that landed here goes to its
        # sheath, not the default stow container; STOW mops up anything else.
        fput "store left"
        fput "stow left" if checkleft
      end
      get_instrument
      false
    end
  end

  # Sends the play command, watches the response for the song actually
  # starting and any rank-up messages along the way, then waits for one of
  # the "song ended" messages. Records the completed cycle to STATS_FILE.
  def self.play_and_track
    started_at = Time.now
    rank_ups = 0

    style = pick_style
    fput "play my #{INSTRUMENT} #{style}"

    give_up_at = Time.now + SONG_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
    recent = []        # last few non-empty lines of anything
    about_it = []      # last few lines that mention the instrument

    loop do
      line = get?
      if line.nil?
        if Time.now > give_up_at
          Lich::Messaging.msg("bold", "[Playsongs] No song-ending message for #{SONG_TIMEOUT_SECONDS}s -- assuming it ended and playing again. " \
                                      "If this keeps happening, the ending text is new: add it to STOP_REGEX. Last lines about the #{INSTRUMENT}: #{(about_it.empty? ? recent : about_it).join(' | ')}")
          record_rank_ups_only(rank_ups, advance_style: true)
          return
        end
        sleep 0.1
        next
      end

      stripped = line.strip
      unless stripped.empty? || stripped =~ /\A[<>]/
        recent << stripped[0, 120]
        recent.shift while recent.length > 5
        # a bard's song-magic spam can bury the real ending in `recent`
        if stripped.downcase.include?(INSTRUMENT.split.last)
          about_it << stripped[0, 160]
          about_it.shift while about_it.length > 4
        end
      end

      if line =~ INVALID_STYLE_REGEX
        if PINNED_STYLE
          echo "#{INSTRUMENT} has no '#{style}' style (#{line.strip}) -- stopping. Run it without a style to cycle the ones it does have."
          exit
        end
        Lich::Messaging.msg("bold", "[Playsongs] #{INSTRUMENT} rejected the '#{style}' style (#{line.strip}) -- skipping to the next one.")
        record_rank_ups_only(rank_ups, advance_style: true)
        return
      end
      if line =~ TWO_HANDED_REGEX
        echo "#{INSTRUMENT} needs both hands -- putting the right hand away and retrying."
        # STORE (not STOW): routes a readied weapon to its sheath per the
        # in-game STORE config instead of dumping the runestaff in the default
        # stow container. Fall back to STOW for anything STORE doesn't handle so
        # the hand always ends up clear (otherwise this retries forever).
        fput "store right"
        fput "stow right" if checkright
        started_at = Time.now
        give_up_at = started_at + SONG_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
        fput "play my #{INSTRUMENT} #{style}"
        next
      end
      if line =~ START_REGEX
        started_at = Time.now
        give_up_at = started_at + SONG_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
      end
      if line =~ RANKUP_REGEX
        rank_ups += 1
        Lich::Messaging.msg("bold", "[Playsongs] Rank up! #{line.strip}")
      end
      break if line =~ STOP_REGEX
    end

    # Round once and use that consistently below -- comparing the raw float
    # against MIN_SONG_SECONDS while displaying the rounded value could
    # print e.g. "300s ... under 300s" for a song that was really 299.6s.
    duration = (Time.now - started_at).round

    if duration < MIN_SONG_SECONDS - SONG_LENGTH_TOLERANCE
      record_rank_ups_only(rank_ups)
      echo "Song aborted after #{duration}s (under #{MIN_SONG_SECONDS}s) -- not counted toward song stats."
    else
      entry = record_song(duration, rank_ups, style)
      summary = "[Playsongs] Song complete in #{duration}s (#{style}, rank-ups: #{rank_ups}). #{INSTRUMENT} totals: #{entry['songs_played']} songs, #{entry['rank_ups']} rank-ups, #{format_duration(entry['total_seconds'])} played."
      # echo/respond force <output class="mono"/> and HTML-escape <, > --
      # which mangles pushBold/popBold into inert text. Lich::Messaging.msg
      # goes through _respond instead (no mono-wrap/escaping), so the color
      # tags actually survive to the client.
      Lich::Messaging.msg("bold", summary)
    end
  end

  # ";playsongs help"/";playsongs stats" work standalone (before the script
  # is even running) since vars[1] resolved to a command instead of an
  # instrument up above. "pause"/"resume" don't make sense without a
  # running instance to control, so just say so instead of starting one.
  if EARLY_COMMAND
    case EARLY_COMMAND
    when 'stats'
      show_stats
    when 'help'
      show_help
    when 'pause', 'resume', 'tune'
      echo "Not running yet -- start with ;playsongs [instrument] first, then use 'playsongs #{EARLY_COMMAND}' (no semicolon) once it's going."
    end
    exit
  end

  echo "Playsongs started -- instrument: #{INSTRUMENT}, style: #{PINNED_STYLE || 'cycling soft/fast/slow'}"

  # Lets typing "playsongs <command>" (no leading semicolon) control the
  # running script without being sent upstream as a game command.
  # Registered as a plain UpstreamHook (not tied to this script's own loop)
  # so it still works while playsongs is already running in the background
  # -- ";playsongs stats" would just hit Lich's "already running" guard
  # instead.
  UpstreamHook.remove('playsongs-trigger') if UpstreamHook.list.include?('playsongs-trigger')
  UpstreamHook.add('playsongs-trigger', proc { |client_string|
    match = client_string.strip.match(/^(?:<c>)?playsongs\s+(\S+)$/i)
    if match
      word = match[1].downcase
      command = Playsongs.match_command(word)
      case command
      when 'stats'
        Playsongs.show_stats
      when 'help'
        Playsongs.show_help
      when 'pause'
        Playsongs.pause!
        respond '[Playsongs] Pausing after the current song finishes. Resume with: playsongs resume'
      when 'resume'
        # Just flips the flag -- fput (and anything that calls it, like
        # ensure_in_left_hand) requires Script.current, which is nil from
        # this UpstreamHook callback since it runs on Lich's input thread,
        # not the script's own thread. The main loop (running on the
        # script's actual thread) picks up the cleared flag within
        # PAUSE_POLL_SECONDS, prints its own "playing again in Ns" countdown,
        # and does the hand check/fix and play from there instead.
        Playsongs.resume!
      when 'tune'
        # Same reason as resume: only flips a flag; the main loop does the TUNE.
        Playsongs.request_tune!
        respond '[Playsongs] Will tune before the next song.'
      else
        respond "[Playsongs] Unknown command '#{word}'. Try: playsongs help"
      end
      nil
    else
      client_string
    end
  })

  # Stow the instrument on the way out regardless of how this script stops --
  # killed by fizzdusk between arena runs, killed manually (;k playsongs), or an
  # uncaught error -- so it never gets left sitting in hand. Also cleans up the
  # trigger hook so it doesn't linger registered after the script's gone (Lich
  # warns about exactly that otherwise).
  before_dying {
    UpstreamHook.remove('playsongs-trigger') if UpstreamHook.list.include?('playsongs-trigger')
    stow_instrument
  }

  if ensure_in_left_hand
    echo "Already holding #{INSTRUMENT} in my left hand, skipping get."
  end

  loop do
    blocker = blocking_script
    if blocker
      if @blocking_script != blocker
        echo "Pausing while #{blocker} is running."
        @blocking_script = blocker
        stow_instrument
      end
      sleep PAUSE_POLL_SECONDS
      next
    elsif @blocking_script
      echo "#{@blocking_script} finished -- resuming."
      @blocking_script = nil
    end

    if paused?
      stow_instrument unless @was_paused
      @was_paused = true
      sleep PAUSE_POLL_SECONDS
      next
    elsif @was_paused
      @was_paused = false
      echo "Resumed -- playing #{INSTRUMENT} again in #{RESTART_PAUSE_SECONDS} seconds..."
      sleep RESTART_PAUSE_SECONDS
    end

    # The game sometimes auto-stows the instrument between songs (e.g. hands
    # needed for something else), which also fires the same "song ended"
    # messages this script watches for -- so always confirm it's still
    # correctly in the left hand before trying to play again instead of
    # assuming the last song ended because it actually finished.
    ensure_in_left_hand
    maybe_tune
    play_and_track
    @song_count += 1
    maybe_check_skill(@song_count)
    sleep RESTART_PAUSE_SECONDS
  end

end #End of module
